  "minutes": 25,
  "cards": [
   [
    "An Atlantic, mid-latitude setting",
    "The UK lies in the mid-latitudes beside the Atlantic and close to continental Europe. Prevailing westerly and southwesterly airflows often bring moist maritime air. Ocean influence moderates temperatures, especially coastal winters. Different air masses and changing pressure systems bring variation; prevailing does not mean the wind always blows from one direction."
   ],
   [
    "Explain the broad patterns",
    "Southern places are generally warmer than northern ones; altitude also lowers temperature. Western uplands are often wetter: moist air rises over relief, cools and condenses, while sheltered eastern areas can be drier. Coastlines, exposure and seasonal land–sea contrasts modify simple north–south or west–east rules."
   ],
   [
    "Compare controls, not slogans",
    "Inland areas can have greater seasonal temperature ranges than coasts. Western maritime influence may make winters milder than at an eastern place of similar latitude, while summer differences can reverse. UK conditions reflect latitude, altitude, relief, prevailing wind and ocean proximity together. This is a conceptual comparison, not a current weather map or numerical climate normal."
   ]
  ],
